One query, three versions.

```csharp
public record TicketSummary(string Id, string Subject);

// Transforms every ticket, then throws most of the work away.
var wasteful = tickets
    .Select(t => new TicketSummary(t.Id, t.Subject.ToUpperInvariant()))
    .Where(s => s.Subject.Contains("PRINTER"));

// Filters first, so only the survivors are transformed.
var better = tickets
    .Where(t => t.Subject.Contains("printer", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
    .Select(t => new TicketSummary(t.Id, t.Subject));
```

And the element operators, chosen deliberately:

```csharp
// Two tickets with one id would mean corrupt data — say so.
var ticket = tickets.Single(t => t.Id == id);

// Several may match; I want the earliest, and none is normal.
var oldestOpen = tickets
    .Where(t => t.Status == TicketStatus.Open)
    .OrderBy(t => t.OpenedAt)
    .FirstOrDefault();

if (oldestOpen is null)
{
    return NoBreaches();
}
```

Note what `FirstOrDefault` buys here: the absence of an open ticket is an ordinary outcome, and the `is null` check handles it immediately rather than letting a default travel onward. Had the element type been a value type, the default would be `0` rather than null — and a `0` that means "nothing matched" is indistinguishable from a `0` that is real data.

