items.promptMarkdown:
```csharp
var costs = new List<decimal>();

Console.WriteLine(costs.Sum());
Console.WriteLine(costs.Count());
Console.WriteLine(costs.Average());
```

Assume the program is run exactly as written. What does it print before it stops? Type the lines it prints, then on the next line write `throws` if it terminates with an exception.

solution.code:
function groupCounts(rows, keyField) {
  const counts = {};
  for (const row of rows) {
    let key = 'unknown';
    if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(row, keyField) && row[keyField] !== undefined && row[keyField] !== null) {
      key = String(row[keyField]);
    }
    if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(counts, key)) {
      counts[key] = counts[key] + 1;
    } else {
      counts[key] = 1;
    }
  }
  const out = [];
  for (const key of Object.keys(counts)) {
    out.push({ key: key, count: counts[key] });
  }
  out.sort(function (a, b) {
    if (a.key < b.key) {
      return -1;
    }
    if (a.key > b.key) {
      return 1;
    }
    return 0;
  });
  return out;
}

grading.starterCode:
function groupCounts(rows, keyField) {
  // Counts into an object and returns it without ordering or the unknown bucket.
  const counts = {};
  for (const row of rows) {
    const key = row[keyField];
    counts[key] = (counts[key] || 0) + 1;
  }
  return Object.keys(counts).map(function (k) {
    return { key: k, count: counts[k] };
  });
}

items.workedExampleMarkdown:
Deferral, demonstrated three ways.

```csharp
// 1. Nothing has executed yet — this line only builds a description.
var open = tickets.Where(t => { Console.WriteLine($"testing {t.Id}"); return t.IsOpen; });

Console.WriteLine("query built");   // prints FIRST, before any "testing" line
var list = open.ToList();           // now the predicate runs, once per ticket
var again = open.ToList();          // and runs again, from the start

// 2. The source is read at execution time.
var names = new List<string> { "rowan" };
var query = names.Select(n => n.ToUpperInvariant());
names.Add("ash");
foreach (var n in query) { Console.WriteLine(n); }   // ROWAN, ASH — both

// 3. Short-circuiting means the predicate may not run at all.
var firstOpen = tickets.Where(IsOpen).Take(2).ToList();
// stops as soon as two matches are found; later tickets are never tested
```

The third case is the one that makes counting predicate calls a genuinely useful skill: a chain ending in `Take` or `First` walks only as far as it must. That is what makes deferral a performance feature rather than merely a hazard — it lets a filter over a million elements stop after finding three.

The fix for the first case is a single, deliberate materialisation:

```csharp
var openTickets = tickets.Where(t => t.IsOpen).ToList();  // executed once, here
```

items.promptMarkdown:
This method is supposed to record which tickets were already breaching when the sweep began, then process everything.

```csharp
public void Sweep(List<Ticket> tickets)
{
    var breachedAtStart = tickets.Where(t => t.IsBreached);

    foreach (var t in tickets.ToList())
    {
        Process(t);          // may set IsBreached on a ticket
    }

    Report(breachedAtStart.Count());   // reports far more than expected
}
```

Why does the report include tickets that were not breaching at the start?

solution.code:
function predicateCalls(statuses, wanted, take) {
  if (take === 0) {
    return 0;
  }
  let calls = 0;
  let matches = 0;
  for (const status of statuses) {
    calls = calls + 1;
    if (status === wanted) {
      matches = matches + 1;
      if (take !== null && matches >= take) {
        return calls;
      }
    }
  }
  return calls;
}

